WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.833
Inko is making a game,
and this slime needs two changes.

2
00:00:03.833 --> 00:00:06.833
Stretch it twice as wide,
and give it a quarter turn.

3
00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:10.333
Stretch first, then turn,
and the slime stands tall.

4
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:14.367
Turn first, then stretch,
and it lies wide.

5
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:17.400
Same two steps, two different slimes.

6
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.067
Each step is a matrix.

7
00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:23.100
Today we'll open one up, and see
why the order changes everything.

8
00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:27.133
A matrix is a machine for space.

9
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:30.800
Feed it a vector, and it
hands back another vector.

10
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:33.567
One rule moves every point
of the plane at once,

11
00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:37.633
and that rule is strict:
straight lines stay straight,

12
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:41.433
parallel lines stay parallel,
and the origin never moves.

13
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:43.167
That's what linear means.

14
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:47.700
Bend the lines, the way a fisheye
lens does, and it's no longer linear:

15
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.867
no matrix can do that.

16
00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:53.333
Slide the whole plane,
and the origin moves:

17
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:58.100
a 2x2 matrix can't do that,
not until the next lesson.

18
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:04.000
Because the grid stays evenly spaced,
two arrows decide everything.

19
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:07.433
î is the vector (1, 0).

20
00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:11.267
And ĵ is the vector (0, 1).

21
00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:14.667
Know where those two land,
and you know the whole transform.

22
00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:19.033
A 2x2 matrix stores exactly
that, in its columns.

23
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:21.700
The first column is where î lands.

24
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.333
The second is where ĵ lands.

25
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:28.200
Here, the first column
is the vector (2, 0).

26
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.367
So î doubles along x.

27
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:33.933
The second is the vector (0, 1).

28
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:36.100
So ĵ stays put.

29
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.467
That's a horizontal stretch,
read without multiplying anything.

30
00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:45.133
Columns, not rows: that's
the rule people get wrong.

31
00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:48.500
So how does it move any other vector?

32
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.367
Every vector is already a mix of î and ĵ.

33
00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:58.300
The vector (3, 4) is three î plus four ĵ.

34
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:02.600
The matrix keeps those weights,
and swaps in the new arrows.

35
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.233
The first column is the vector (2, 0).

36
00:02:06.233 --> 00:02:11.467
Take three of it. The second
column is the vector (0, 1).

37
00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:16.367
Take four of it. Together
they give the vector (6, 4).

38
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.667
The row-by-column recipe agrees,
but columns show you why.

39
00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:24.233
Now let's build a rotation from scratch.

40
00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:28.400
Turn the plane by an angle,
θ, counter-clockwise.

41
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.467
î starts on the circle of
radius one, at angle zero.

42
00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:36.867
Turned by θ, it lands on
the vector (cos θ, sin θ).

43
00:02:36.867 --> 00:02:38.433
That's column one.

44
00:02:38.433 --> 00:02:42.400
ĵ starts at 90°, so it ends at 90° + θ.

45
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.200
That's the vector (−sin θ, cos θ).

46
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.400
That's column two. Check at 90°.

47
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.333
î swings up to the vector (0, 1).

48
00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:55.833
ĵ swings left to the vector (−1, 0).

49
00:02:55.833 --> 00:02:59.467
Drop that minus sign,
and the two axes just swap places.

50
00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:03.133
The shape comes out mirrored,
and no rotation can do that.

51
00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:06.633
A scale matrix is even simpler.

52
00:03:06.633 --> 00:03:11.133
The x scale sits in the first column,
the y scale in the second,

53
00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:14.133
and each axis stretches on its own.

54
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.267
A shear is different. î stays put,

55
00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:21.567
but ĵ slides over to the vector (k, 1).

56
00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:24.833
The square leans,
and its base never moves.

57
00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:27.767
That looks like a small
rotation, but it isn't.

58
00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:31.200
A rotation keeps every
edge the same length.

59
00:03:31.233 --> 00:03:35.867
The sheared edge grows, to √(k² + 1).

60
00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.867
Back to the two slimes.

61
00:03:38.867 --> 00:03:41.767
A chain of matrices
runs from right to left:

62
00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:44.533
the matrix next to the vector runs first.

63
00:03:44.533 --> 00:03:50.300
So stretch first, then turn,
is written R * S * v.

64
00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.900
Read it left to right like a sentence,
and you get the order backwards.

65
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:59.900
Watch î. R * S stretches it
first, to the vector (2, 0).

66
00:04:00.367 --> 00:04:04.400
Then it turns it, to the vector (0, 2).

67
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.300
S * R turns it first,
to the vector (0, 1).

68
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.200
Then the stretch along x can't touch it.

69
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:17.100
Same two matrices, different
answers, and no error anywhere.

70
00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.267
Stretched along the wrong axis?

71
00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:21.333
Check the order first.

72
00:04:22.067 --> 00:04:25.033
So: a matrix moves the whole plane,

73
00:04:25.033 --> 00:04:28.000
keeping lines straight
and the origin still.

74
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:34.000
Its columns are where î and ĵ land, and
times a vector, it mixes those columns.

75
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.200
Rotate, scale and shear
are just different columns,

76
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.833
and in a chain, the matrix
next to the vector runs first.

77
00:04:41.833 --> 00:04:46.100
3x3 and 4x4 matrices
follow the same rules.

78
00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.167
Next time, one extra dimension
lets them slide things, too.

79
00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:54.600
Now open the 2x2 matrix demo
in this lesson, drag only b,

80
00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.867
and see which arrow moves.

81
00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.767
If this clicked, give it a like,

82
00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:01.533
and subscribe so you don't
miss the next lesson.

83
00:05:01.533 --> 00:05:03.167
See you there!
